 "drivingQuestion": "Why may priests eat all offerings in any manner of preparation?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "The verse supplies the reason.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.91a.19",
   "daf": "91a",
   "sentenceId": 18,
   "he": "וּבְכוּלָּן כֹּהֲנִים רַשָּׁאִין כּוּ׳. מַאי טַעְמָא? אָמַר קְרָא ״לְמׇשְׁחָה״ – לִגְדוּלָּה, כְּדֶרֶךְ שֶׁהַמְּלָכִים אוֹכְלִין.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And with regard to all of» the offerings that are eaten, «the priests are permitted» to alter the manner of their consumption and eat them as they choose. The Gemara asks: «What is the reason» for this halakha? The Gemara answers: The «verse states,» with regard to the gifts of the priesthood: “Of all the consecrated items of the children of Israel to you have I given them «lemoshḥa”» (Numbers 18:8). Targum Onkelos renders this term as: «For greatness,» indicating that the flesh of the offerings should be eaten «in» the «manner that kings eat» their food, i.e., prepared in any way they want."
  }
 ],
